that AWS’s current documentation says **DeepSeek V3.2 supports Converse** and is available in `us-east-2`, so `ValidationException: Operation not allowed` usually points to one of two things: either **account/model access is not actually enabled for invocation in that region**, or there is an **IAM/service-policy restriction** even though model discovery works. AWS notes that Converse ultimately requires `bedrock:InvokeModel`. citeturn529571search0turn529571search2turn529571search3\n\nLet’s isolate that with the smallest next test: use **InvokeModel** instead of Converse. AWS documents DeepSeek V3.2 on `bedrock-runtime` with model ID `deepseek.v3.2`, and provides an InvokeModel example for exactly this model. citeturn529571search1\n\nCreate a BOM-free request body:\n\n```powershell\n$body = @'\n{\n  \"messages\": [\n    {\n      \"role\": \"user\",\n      \"content\": \"Reply exactly: BEDROCK DEEPSEEK OK\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"max_tokens\": 128\n}\n'@\n\n[System.IO.File]::WriteAllText(\n  \"$env:TEMP\\deepseek-body.json\",\n  $body,\n  (New-Object System.Text.UTF8Encoding($false))\n)\n```\n\nThen run:\n\n```powershell\naws bedrock-runtime invoke-model `\n  --region us-east-2 `\n  --model-id deepseek.v3.2 `\n  --content-type application/json `\n  --accept application/json `\n  --body \"fileb://$env:TEMP\\deepseek-body.json\" `\n  \"$env:TEMP\\deepseek-response.json\"\n```\n\nThen:\n\n```powershell\nGet-Content \"$env:TEMP\\deepseek-response.json\"\n```\n\nThis gives us a clean fork:\n\n- **If InvokeModel works:** your Bedrock access is valid and the issue is specifically the Converse path. We can still wire OpenCode using a supported Bedrock/OpenAI-compatible route.\n- **If InvokeModel also says `Operation not allowed`:** then this is an account/access-policy problem, not Talon and not OpenCode.\n\nOne more important thing: model listing only proves the model is **catalog-visible**, not necessarily that your IAM principal can invoke it. So this test will settle 