h is simpler: the credentials are dead with the old architecture, and you’re rebuilding the credential model anyway. The only thing that still matters operationally is not accidentally preserving stale secrets into the new world.\n\nSo for Don/Coda, I’d tighten the rule going forward:\n\n**For retired legacy trees, old credentials are not a blocker to cleanup unless they are known to still power a live system.** Otherwise:\n- don’t rotate them just to justify deletion;\n- don’t preserve them in normal archive;\n- don’t let one `.env` turn into another reconciliation project;\n- classify them **retired / do-not-reuse** and remove them with the legacy estate.\n\nThe one real exception you just named is **Edge Crew**, and even there, you’re not relying on the old state because OpenRouter still needs to be set back up. So we should handle Edge Crew credentials as a separate, intentional rebuild—not as a reason to preserve old credential sprawl.\n\nAnd yes, since Talon is already executing his current path, I would **not interrupt him with another prompt**. Let that wave finish. When we review his return, we apply this simpler ruling and stop spawning tiny exception loops."]}, "create_time": 1789529101.426208, "end_turn": true, "id": "5d1ea44d-7cad-4fd7-bcf3-6b418d3132ec", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [], "content_references": [], "conversation_followup_suggestions_eligible": true, "cot_version": "v5", "default_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "finish_details": {"stop_tokens": [200002], "type": "stop"}, "is_complete": true, "message_type": "next", "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "model_switcher_deny": [], "parent_id": "8c15ed0a-2df5-48ac-9ca8-e6acb07dbcf9", "request_id": "931268d6-4a32-4b41-876f-c97212fc6429", "resolved_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "search_result_groups": [], "story_events": [], "thinking_effort": "standard", "turn_exchange_id": "b6cb2cca-c947-40dc-bdec-d7e9f033657d", "turn_id": "a03282c9-1b61-459b-aa96-83e5bdf20f07", "working_turn_i