ontent_type": "text", "parts": ["Exactly. That gives us the cleanup rule we were missing.\n\nThe active environment should be treated as an **allowlist**, not as “keep everything unless we can prove it is junk.”\n\nRight now, Don’s trusted/live set is basically:\n\n- **VoiceBridge**\n- **Web scraper**\n- **Command Center**\n- **DevPad**\n- **Roundtable / crowdsourcing**\n- **Amazon Bedrock**\n- **Projects**, already organized:\n  - Edge Crew\n  - Casa Companion\n  - other current project roots we explicitly recognize\n\nEverything else should start from:\n\n**RETIRE / DELETE / MANIFEST-ONLY unless there is a current live dependency or Don explicitly says keep it.**\n\nThat means:\n- old Lares_Family variants: go\n- old duplicate VoiceBridge/web-scraper/command-center copies inside Lares: go\n- old Azure material: garbage\n- obsolete Claude/Kimi/DeepSeek environments: garbage\n- stale experimental roots: garbage\n- duplicate configs/runtime homes: garbage\n- old versions that grep/search could accidentally rediscover: especially important to remove\n\nAnd your grep point is the key operational reason. Even if an old folder is “harmless,” it is **not harmless to an AI/tooling environment** if models search broadly and find obsolete specs, configs, prompts, or code and mistake them for authority.\n\nSo the cleanup objective is now sharper:\n\n> **Reduce the searchable filesystem to current truth plus intentional archive evidence.**\n\nNot “preserve every historical copy.”\n\nFor anything retired, we should keep at most a lightweight archive ledger:\n\n`NAME | FORMER PURPOSE | RETIRED DATE | CURRENT SUCCESSOR | RESTORE VALUE`\n\nIf restore value is low or none, **delete the payload**.\n\nThat is the rule I’d apply to the rest of this cleanup."]}, "create_time": 1789529231.408883, "end_turn": true, "id": "86a485a4-f104-4e82-b8c9-7197e28e60c6", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [], "content_references": [], "conversation_followup_suggestions_eligible": true, "co