rom this set:\n>\n> - Kalshi\n> - Falcon\n> - Calibro\n> - Chiave\n> - Corrado\n> - VoiceBridge\n> - DevPad\n> - OpenCode\n> - Bedrock\n> - NetSuite\n> - Big Pickle\n> - Casa Companion\n>\n> Do not duplicate terms already present.\n>\n> Do not change model/provider/ASR settings.\n>\n> ## 4. TESTS\n>\n> Verify:\n>\n> `Kashi probability` → `Kalshi probability`\n>\n> `Calci` → `Kalshi`\n>\n> `Calcium` remains `Calcium`\n>\n> `Calcify` remains `Calcify`\n>\n> `Cauchy distribution` remains `Cauchy distribution`\n>\n> `API key` remains `API key`\n>\n> `capisce` remains `capisce`\n>\n> Existing:\n>\n> `Luce`\n>\n> `Coda`\n>\n> `Chiave`\n>\n> `Calibro`\n>\n> remain correct.\n>\n> ## 5. RETURN\n>\n> `WORD-BOUNDARY SUPPORT ADDED: YES/NO`\n>\n> `KASHI RULE: PASS/FAIL`\n>\n> `CALCI RULE: PASS/FAIL`\n>\n> `CALCIUM COLLISION TEST: PASS/FAIL`\n>\n> `CAUCHY COLLISION TEST: PASS/FAIL`\n>\n> `KEY COLLISION TEST: PASS/FAIL`\n>\n> `KEYTERMS ADDED`\n>\n> `FILES MODIFIED`\n>\n> `DEVIATIONS`\n>\n> Then STOP.\n>\n> ## HARD GUARDRAILS\n>\n> - no LLM proofreading redesign\n> - no speculative mappings\n> - no fuzzy matching\n> - no phonetic matching engine\n> - no archive scan\n> - no unrelated VoiceBridge changes\n\nThat’s the right size. We improve what the logs actually proved and keep operating. Then the next few days of logs will tell us what the **next** real misses are."]}, "create_time": 1789611097.759223, "end_turn": true, "id": "b345e754-a577-4ab4-9cfd-c3ba490f2980", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [], "content_references": [], "conversation_followup_suggestions_eligible": true, "cot_version": "v5", "default_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "finish_details": {"stop_tokens": [200002], "type": "stop"}, "is_complete": true, "message_type": "next", "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "model_switcher_deny": [], "parent_id": "197067fc-c711-4326-85e5-84f1e0a6dd94", "request_id": "9af421c6-9962-40d9-a33a-f18d13ed9e2b", "resolved_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "search_result_groups": [], "story_events": [], "thinking_effort": "standard", "turn_exchange_id": "dd33517e-8dae-40a2-9625-2e4ae46fd3de", "turn_id": "97729e54-4a52-4573-a889-49ea251150f7", "working_turn_id": "dd33517e-8dae-40a2-9625-2e4ae46fd3de"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1789611113.265699, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": null, "content": {"content": "Worked for a few seconds", "content_type": "reasoning_recap"}, "create_time": 1789611100.922724, "end_turn": false, "id": "197067fc-c711-4326-85e5-84f1e0a6dd94", "metadata": {"can_save": false, "conversation_followup_suggestions_eligible": true, "cot_version": "v5", "default_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "finished_duration_sec": 3, "hide_inline_actions": true, "message_type": "next", "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "model_switcher_deny": [], "parent_id": "7014d262-6c3b-4700-afb3-ac5037828bd6", "reasoning_end_time": 1789611100.886355, "reasoning_recap_type": "hide_all", "reasoning_start_time": 1789611097.8051472, "reasoning_status": "reasoning_ended", "request_id": "9af421c6-9962-40d9-a33a-f18d13ed9e2b", "resolved_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "thinking_effort": "standard", "turn_exchange_id": "dd33517e-8dae-40a2-9625-2e4ae46fd3de", "turn_id": "97729e54-4a52-4573-a889-49ea251150f7", "working_turn_id": "dd33517e-8dae-40a2-9625-2e4ae46fd3de"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": null, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "user"}, "channel": null, "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["Yeah, but my, my goal is to find all the ones that look off. Right, so my goal is to— now it's moving a little bit faster— is to autocorrect stuff like capisce. "]}, "create_time": 1789611134.439, "end_turn": null, "id": "5b00f3b7-0808-4119-a670-6195df8772a4", "metadata": {"can_save": false, "message_source": null, "request_id": "e38be5d8-b48b-46c1-aaaa-29eeff32f352", "selected_sources": [], "serialization_metadata": {"custom_symbol_offsets": []}, "turn_exchange_id": "7b984de3-29ff-4947-bcfe-473b4bf6c6d0", "turn_id": "2fd0eb7d-1a29-4d4b-a766-a5cc34cf2b48", "working_turn_id": "7b984de3-29ff-4947-bcfe-473b4bf6c6d0"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1789611152.616304, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": "final", "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["Right — that changes the objective.\n\nYou’re not trying to only patch the **already-proven recurring misses**. You want VoiceBridge to actively identify **anything that looks linguistically wrong or domain-wrong**, then normalize obvious cases automatically when confidence is high.\n\nSo the next recon should look for three classes:\n\n- words that are **clearly malformed** relative to your known vocabulary\n- words that are **valid English/Italian/etc. but wrong in context**\n- words that are **phonetically close to one of your canonical terms**\n\n`capisce` is a good example of the nuance: if ASR already gives `capisce`, there’s nothing to fix. But if it outputs `capeesh`, `capish`, `capiche`, etc., those should probably normalize to your preferred canonical spelling automatically.\n\nI’d have Big Pickle do a broader **anomaly-detection pass**, not just frequency counting:\n\n> # BIG PICKLE — VOICEBRIDGE TRANSCRIPTION ANOMALY + AUTO-PROOF RECON\n>\n> **MODE:** bounded recon only  \n> **OBJECTIVE:** identify suspicious transcription outputs that are likely wrong even when they occur only once, then classify which can be safely auto-normalized.\n>\n> ## GOAL\n>\n> Do not restrict analysis to repeated errors.\n>\n> Find:\n>\n> - malformed-looking tokens\n> - phonetic near-matches to canonical GCE/tool/company vocabulary\n> - unusual capitalization/spelling\n> - improbable words in local context\n> - colloquial phrases with inconsistent spellings\n> - one-off errors that are obvious from surrounding sentence context\n>\n> ## CANONICAL VOCABULARY\n>\n> Use the current live framework/tool vocabulary as reference, including:\n>\n> Luce, Coda, Talon, Chiave, Calibro, Corrado, Ragno, Falcon, Keel, Kimi, Kalshi, VoiceBridge, OpenCode, DevPad, Bedrock, NetSuite, Edge Crew, Big Pickle, Casa Companion, EC8, GCE.\n>\n> Do not assume every near-match should be corrected.\n>\n> ## COLLOQUIAL NORMALIZATION\n>\n> Specifically inspect variants around phrases Don commonly says, including:\n>\n> `capisce`\n>\n> Look for forms such as:\n>\n> `capeesh`\n> `capish`\n> `capiche`\n> `capisce`\n> and phonetic equivalents.\n>\n> Return observed variants and recommend one canonical output spelling.\n>\n> ## ANALYSIS METHOD\n>\n> Review recent raw/final transcription logs and flag suspicious tokens even at count=1.\n>\n> For each candidate return:\n>\n> `RAW FORM`\n>\n> `LIKELY INTENDED FORM`\n>\n> `COUNT`\n>\n> `EXAMPLE CONTEXT`\n>\n> `WHY IT LOOKS WRONG`\n>\n> `CONFIDENCE`\n>\n> `SAFE TO AUTO-CORRECT?`\n>\n> `COLLISION RISK`\n>\n> ## CLASSIFY\n>\n> Put every candidate into one of:\n>\n> `SAFE AUTO-NORMALIZE`\n>\n> `CONTEXTUAL / NEEDS RULE`\n>\n> `WATCHLIST`\n>\n> `DO NOT TOUCH`\n>\n> ## IMPORTANT\n>\n> A candidate can be SAFE even if it occurred once, if the context and canonical vocabulary make the intent unambiguous.\n>\n> Conversely, a frequent form is not automatically safe if it can be legitimate English/domain language.\n>\n> ## RETURN\n>\n> `TOP SUSPICIOUS FORMS`\n>\n> `SAFE AUTO-NORMALIZE`\n>\n> `CONTEXTUAL`\n>\n> `WATCHLIST`\n>\n> `DO NOT TOUCH`\n>\n> `COLLOQUIAL NORMALIZATION DECISIONS`\n>\n> `RECOMMENDED NEXT RULESET`\n>\n> Then STOP.\n>\n> No code changes, no glossary edits, no config edits.\n\nThat gets you closer to the actual goal: **proofread like a smart editor, not just a typo counter**."]}, "create_time": 1789611136.085789, "end_turn": true, "id": "69df7e00-8c45-45e3-ade3-cc19cc1c5e16", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [], "content_references": [], "conversat