r Brave/Coda, we should create a dedicated Brave app-window or shortcut whose window title is predictably `Coda`, rather than trying to target a generic Brave process.\n\nSo I’d slightly revise the implementation goal:\n\n**VoiceBridge should resolve a canonical destination name → find the matching titled window → focus it.**\n\nNot terminal-specific. Generic window targeting.\n\nAnd yes, `approve Coda` / `approve Luce` should be a second command family once focus is reliable. That can simply send the appropriate approval keystroke or click behavior to the now-focused destination, but we should first inspect what “approve” actually means in each UI so we don’t hard-code the wrong action.\n\nThe next Big Pickle pass should therefore be:\n\n> # BIG PICKLE — NAMED DESTINATION CONTROL RECON\n>\n> **MODE:** bounded recon only\n>\n> **OBJECTIVE:** define and verify a generic named-destination model for VoiceBridge across terminal and browser/app windows.\n>\n> ## TARGET MODEL\n>\n> Voice commands should resolve:\n>\n> `go to <name>`\n>\n> where `<name>` is a canonical destination such as:\n>\n> - Luce\n> - Talon\n> - Coda\n> - DevPad\n> - VoiceBridge\n>\n> Destination type may be:\n>\n> - Windows Terminal/OpenCode window\n> - Brave browser/app window\n> - desktop app\n>\n> VoiceBridge should not care about app type once a stable window title exists.\n>\n> ## 1. CURRENT WINDOW TITLE INVENTORY\n>\n> Inspect currently running windows and return:\n>\n> `WINDOW TITLE`\n>\n> `PROCESS`\n>\n> `CANONICAL DESTINATION`\n>\n> `TITLE STABLE?`\n>\n> `UNIQUE?`\n>\n> Specifically inspect:\n>\n> - current OpenCode/terminal windows\n> - Brave window containing Coda/ChatGPT\n> - DevPad\n> - VoiceBridge\n>\n> ## 2. CODA / BRAVE TARGETING\n>\n> Determine the cleanest way to create a dedicated Coda browser destination with a stable title.\n>\n> Prefer, in order:\n>\n> 1. dedicated Brave app/PWA window with stable title\n> 2. dedicated Brave shortcut/profile/window\n> 3. title-based browser-window matching\n>\n> Do not rely on tab order.\n>\n> Return smallest reliable method.\n>\n> ## 3. GENERIC NAMED FOCUS\n>\n> Confirm whether existing `_list_visible_windows` + `_activate_window` can support:\n>\n> `focus_named_destination(name)`\n>\n> across any visible Windows window, regardless of process type.\n>\n> Recommend exact matching hierarchy:\n>\n> `exact title`\n>\n> then safe unique substring\n>\n> otherwise ambiguity/fail.\n>\n> ## 4. INITIAL DESTINATION REGISTRY\n>\n> Propose a tiny registry:\n>\n> `Luce -> window title`\n>\n> `Talon -> window title`\n>\n> `Coda -> window title`\n>\n> `DevPad -> window title`\n>\n> `VoiceBridge -> window title`\n>\n> No framework redesign.\n>\n> ## 5. APPROVE COMMAND\n>\n> Recon only:\n>\n> Determine what `approve` means in each target UI.\n>\n> Inspect:\n>\n> - OpenCode approval interaction\n> - ChatGPT/Brave approval interaction, if one exists\n>\n> Return:\n>\n> `APPROVE LUCE ACTION`\n>\n> `APPROVE CODA ACTION`\n>\n> `SAME MECHANISM? YES/NO`\n>\n> `SAFE TO VOICE-AUTOMATE? YES/NO`\n>\n> Do not implement approval yet.\n>\n> ## RETURN\n>\n> `NAMED DESTINATION MODEL: VIABLE YES/NO`\n>\n> `CODA WINDOW STRATEGY`\n>\n> `DESTINATION REGISTRY`\n>\n> `GO TO <NAME> IMPLEMENTATION PATH`\n>\n> `APPROVAL ACTION FINDINGS`\n>\n> `SMALLEST NEXT BUILD`\n>\n> Then STOP.\n\nThat gets us to the right architecture: **you name the destination the same way you think about it**, regardless of whether it lives in Terminal, Brave, or another app."]}, "create_time": 1789611630.928027, "end_turn": true, "id": "ecd01471-2ebc-435a-9225-f9b24f38453e", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [], "content_references": [], "conversation_followup_suggestions_eligible": true, "cot_version": "v5", "default_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "finish_details": {"stop_tokens": [200002], "type": "stop"}, "is_complete": true, "message_type": "next", "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "model_switcher_deny": [], "parent_id": "f72e5375-b7e2-45f4-8c81-1ba6905864