b-4119-baf4-f5e8ca94028d", "resolved_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "thinking_effort": "standard", "turn_exchange_id": "b70cd325-84ba-4ce2-9ca5-635000e2fe15", "turn_id": "83b9d625-4e55-4283-b7a4-a459ea17429b", "working_turn_id": "b70cd325-84ba-4ce2-9ca5-635000e2fe15"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": null, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {"real_author": "tool:web"}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": "final", "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["Good — AWS auth is working, and **DeepSeek V3.2 is available in Bedrock**. The important mismatch is your local AWS default region is `us-east-2`, while the command you ran queried `us-east-1`. DeepSeek V3.2 is available in both regions, so we can pick one deliberately; I’d keep this simple and use **`us-east-2` everywhere** to match your configured AWS profile. citeturn409555search1turn409555search2\n\nBefore touching Talon, verify inference directly:\n\n```powershell\naws bedrock-runtime converse `\n  --region us-east-2 `\n  --model-id deepseek.v3.2 `\n  --messages '[{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Reply exactly: BEDROCK DEEPSEEK OK\"}]}]'\n```\n\nIf that returns successfully, Bedrock itself is proven.\n\nThen point OpenCode at Amazon Bedrock. OpenCode supports Bedrock through the AWS credential chain and lets you set the region in `opencode.json`; afterward `/models` should expose the available Bedrock models. citeturn409555search0turn409555search7\n\nUse this minimal config:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"$schema\": \"https://opencode.ai/config.json\",\n  \"provider\": {\n    \"amazon-bedrock\": {\n      \"options\": {\n        \"region\": \"us-east-2\"\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nBecause your normal AWS CLI credentials are already resolving to `PeteW`, you should not need to paste AWS keys into OpenCode. OpenCode can use the same AWS credential chain. citeturn409555search0\n\nThen launch:\n\n```powershell\nopencode\n```\n\nInside OpenCode:\n\n```text\n/models\n```\n\nLook for the Amazon Bedrock provider and **DeepSeek V3.2 / `deepseek.v3.2`**. AWS confirms that exact model ID for the Bedrock runtime. citeturn409555search2\n\nOne thing I would **not** do yet: change Talon bootstrap/config files. First prove this chain:\n\n**AWS credentials → Bedrock V3.2 inference → OpenCode Bedrock provider → plain DeepSeek prompt.**\n\nThen we point Talon at it. That isolates whether our problem is DeepSeek direct-provider nonsense versus Talon/configuration.\n\nRun that `bedrock-runtime converse` command and give me the result."]}, "create_time": 1789413133.426106, "end_turn": true, "id": "24357945-828b-4869-9a43-751aceedd3df", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [], "code_blocks": {"0": {"edited": false, "id": "m338is", "preview_language": null, "previewable": false}, "1": {"edited": false, "id": "dqn91h", "preview_language": null, "previewable": false}, "2": {"edited": false, "id": "zqu88a", "preview_language": null, "previewable": false}, "3": {"edited": false, "id": "j30yxm", "preview_language": null, "previewable": false}}, "content_references": [{"alt": "([AWS Documentation](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/models-region-compatibility.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com))", "end_idx": 407, "error": null, "fallback_items": null, "items": [{"attribution": "AWS Documentation", "attribution_segments": null, "attributions": null, "hue": null, "pub_date": null, "refs": [{"ref_index": 1, "ref_type": "search", "turn_index": 0}, {"ref_index": 2, "ref_type": "search", "turn_index": 0}], "snippet": "", "supporting_websites": [{"attribution": "AWS Documentation", "pub_date": null, "snippet": "", "title": "DeepSeek V3.2 - Amazon Bedrock", "url": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/model-card-deepseek-deepseek-v3-2.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com"}], "title": "Regional availability by models - Amazon Bedrock", "url": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/models-region-c